import hashlib,json,subprocess
from pathlib import Path
W=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]; S=W.parent/'scratch-h3-packet-builder-20261112'; T=S/'composition-a/tree'; O=S/'cross-review'
def pin(p): return {'path':str(p),'sha256':hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest(),'bytes':p.stat().st_size}
def read(p): return json.loads(p.read_bytes())
findings=[
{'id':'H3-X1','grade':'P1','title':'A new packet directory permits reuse of the same delegated direction after failure',
 'location':'src/data_sheets_schema/native_launch_sequence.py:150,165-184; native_subscription_capture.py:360-428; native_capture_words.py:216-219.',
 'scenario':'The only one-start claim is packet/sequence-start.json, and the packet path is caller-selected. After a transient refused capture (not the permanently latched limits-mismatch refusal), invoke the same registration, word and still-fresh direction/host timestamps with a new packet and guard output. The old packet is never consulted; the current word remains reusable and capture allows another slot up to its three-claim bound. Thus a second sequence can reach a later successful capture and launch without the new direction required by N1.3 D12 and N1.8(aa). The attempt-directory one-use gate prevents two completed attempts, but does not prevent this forbidden retry before an attempt exists. Helper2 explicitly notes the direction-reuse limitation; it is not resolved by the packet-exclusive claim.',
 'fix':'Bind a recorded direction identifier and registration digest to one durable start claim in registration-scoped state, independent of packet path, consumed before capture. A permitted restart must present a distinct newly recorded direction; retain the old claim. Add a different-packet retry case after transient capture failure; keep the limits-mismatch permanent refusal separate.',
 'evidence':'Static trace at exact d2efa55014e131f92733bf2c3a90c0830214dc89. test_native_launch_sequence.py:186 tests only reuse of the identical packet after success; it cannot establish one start per direction. No sequence or reservation was executed in this review.'},
{'id':'H3-X2','grade':'P1','title':'Saved script and bundle pin can match disk while different sequence code is running',
 'location':'src/data_sheets_schema/native_launch_sequence.py:21,92,169; src/data_sheets_schema/native_launch_review.py:290-300,346-360.',
 'scenario':'Python loads sequence implementation A, then its source file is replaced by reviewed implementation B before main copies __file__. The retained sequence-script.py contains B, and each fresh child loads the checkout containing B. Current bundle validation hashes B at SOURCE_ROOT, and packet readback also hashes B; neither establishes that the already-loaded parent was B. A can be an older sequence lacking the reviewed stop/time behavior while the child implementation, registration and pinned on-disk B remain valid. The source-location repair avoids verifier-bootstrap imports and checks the file it names, but it does not bind executing parent code to that file. A separate orchestrator invoking the registered child checkout has the same missing association. This is a local code-replacement/startup trust gap, not a hash collision or a historical-bundle bypass.',
 'fix':'Use a trusted launcher that reads and verifies the exact sequence bytes against the bundle before compiling/executing those same captured bytes, retains those exact bytes, and binds the resulting start record into the current capture/launch chain. Do not treat rereading __file__ or the mere presence of a pinned file in the child checkout as executing-code identity. Add an isolated load-A/replace-with-B witness and require refusal before capture.',
 'evidence':'Static data-flow trace. test_native_launch_sequence.py:293 compares retained bytes to an unchanged file read in the test; test_native_review_bundle_v3.py:111 mutates disk and tests disk-pin refusal, not loaded-parent identity. No mutation was made to reviewed source.'},
{'id':'H3-X3','grade':'P2','title':'Parent input checks do not bind the bytes consumed by a child',
 'location':'src/data_sheets_schema/native_launch_sequence.py:84-87,172-185,197-209; src/data_sheets_schema/native_generation.py:208-233.',
 'scenario':'The parent remembers input bytes and checks them immediately before dispatch, but forwards mutable path names without expected digests. Replacement after _unchanged and before the child read gives the child a different internally valid registration/review/word/account-choice set. For example a second valid packet sharing the capture-state directory can cause capture under the replacement word; the parent then rejects its manifest because it is outside the original word slots, but that is after capture has occurred. A stable read inside the child detects changes during that read, not a replacement completed before it. Existing mutation tests change input during one fake step and check refusal before the next; they do not exercise the check-to-child-read window. This finding does not claim a guard for another registration can pass the existing expected guard-digest gate.',
 'fix':'Pass expected input digests from the parent and have each child compare the exact bytes it will use before any effect, or dispatch from immutable captured input objects with equivalent producer/consumer binding. Add a child-start replacement regression that uses another valid input set and proves no capture is reached.',
 'evidence':'Static trace of parent checks versus native_generation reads; no race or capture was run.'}
]
composition=read(S/'composition-a/composition.json')
config=T/'.git/config'; assert pin(config)['sha256']==composition['config_original_sha256']
